{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth his mother acted as regent until he came of age. During this time the County of Tripoli faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal power struggles among the nobility. Raymond was educated in the court of Jerusalem and learned the ways of diplomacy and warfare from an early age.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a key figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a cousin of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was named regent of the kingdom in 1174 when Baldwin IV was still a minor. He held this position for several years and was responsible for defending the kingdom from Saladin’s growing power. His leadership was often criticized for being indecisive and cautious. He was accused of being too eager to negotiate with Muslim rulers instead of engaging in decisive military action. This caused friction with other nobles including the Knights Templar and the Hospitallers. His attempts to secure alliances through marriage and diplomacy were sometimes successful but often undermined by mistrust among the Christian factions.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against rushing into battle against Saladin. He argued that the Christian army should avoid the desert and wait for reinforcements. His advice was ignored and he was sidelined by Guy of Lusignan who took command. After the disastrous defeat at Hattin Raymond was captured along with many other nobles. He was held prisoner for a time but was eventually released. He returned to Tripoli and spent his final years trying to rebuild the shattered defenses of his county. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of a long and turbulent era in the history of the Crusader states. Raymond III remains a complex figure remembered for his caution his diplomatic efforts and his tragic role in the collapse of Christian power in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.3132786452770233}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.002566141774877906}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.4185804502631072e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {